    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nSince 0977f70f4dd3 (#32549), `add_injection()` clips injection ranges to the parent's regions, but it checks for empty ranges before clipping. A range clipped away entirely becomes an empty region, which means \"whole buffer\", and the child then re-injects itself until it overflows the stack.\n\nI hit this as a 100% CPU hang with multi-GB memory growth on a markdown file containing `<script>…</script>`, using the bundled markdown_inline query and nvim-treesitter's html/javascript queries.\n\nClipping first fixes it:\n\n```diff\n local function add_injection(t, pattern, lang, combined, ranges, parent_ranges, result)\n+  ranges = clip_regions(ranges, parent_ranges)\n   if #ranges == 0 then\n@@\n-    table.insert(result[lang], clip_regions(ranges, parent_ranges))\n+    table.insert(result[lang], ranges)\n@@\n-  for _, range in ipairs(clip_regions(ranges, parent_ranges)) do\n+  for _, range in ipairs(ranges) do\n```\n\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n`minimal.lua`:\n\n```lua\nvim.treesitter.query.set('markdown_inline', 'injections', [[\n((code_span) @injection.content\n  (#offset! @injection.content 0 1 0 -1)\n  (#set! injection.language \"lua\")\n  (#set! injection.combined))\n]])\nvim.treesitter.query.set('lua', 'injections', [[\n((string content: (_) @injection.content)\n  (#set! injection.language \"lua\"))\n]])\n```\n\n`repro.md`:\n\n```markdown\n`[[` `]]`\n```\n\n`nvim --clean -u minimal.lua repro.md`\n\n```\nvim.schedule callback: .../runtime/lua/vim/treesitter/languagetree.lua:215: stack overflow\n```\n\nThe two queries recreate the real-world case (markdown_inline's combined html injection, plus html injecting javascript into <script>) using only bundled parsers:\n\n1. markdown_inline injects all code spans as a single combined Lua tree. For ``` `[[` `]]` ``` that tree only includes `[[` and `]]`.\n2. Lua injects Lua into string contents. The long string's content lies between the two code spans, outside the parent's regions, so clipping leaves nothing.\n3. The empty region is treated as \"whole buffer\". The child parses the entire line, finds the string again, and recurses until the stack overflows.\n\nWith `[[x]]` the content falls inside the code span and the nesting stops after one level.\n\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nNo child tree for the clipped-away injection.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nv0.13.0-dev-4c50814\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nN/A (tree-sitter)\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nmacOS 27.0.1\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nRex 0.1.0 (1203)\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nbuild from repo",
